# ThreadSanitizer suppressions for the `native-tsan` job (N2-29; docs/native/rhi.md, "The ThreadSanitizer job").
#
# Used as TSAN_OPTIONS=halt_on_error=1:suppressions=<repo>/tools/native/tsan.supp. The Vulkan loader and the software
# driver the job runs on (lavapipe, from mesa-vulkan-drivers) are not built with ThreadSanitizer, so it cannot see the
# synchronization inside them (their own locks, futexes and thread pool) and could report accesses that are in fact
# ordered, with stacks that end in the library.
#
# They fire when the machine is busy: lavapipe's worker threads, which ThreadSanitizer cannot see synchronising, then
# produce allocator and mutex reports (a data race whose first frame is free, malloc or pthread_mutex_destroy called
# from libvulkan_lvp.so). On a quiet machine (loader 1.3.275, Mesa 25.2.8) the tests happen to pass with an empty file, but
# that is timing; on a loaded one an empty file failed most runs, and with this file they passed.
#
# lavapipe runs LLVM (libLLVM.so.<version>, or libLLVM-<version>.so.1 in older packages; also uninstrumented) on its
# own threads (llvmpipe-N). Native CI run 41252057160 (native at 48f9d2c, 2026-10-31) failed present_test after 312 s
# with a data race whose two access stacks hold only libLLVM.so.20.1 frames under the interceptors (bcmp from
# llvm::detail::IEEEFloat::bitwiseIsEqual against operator new from llvm::allocate_buffer, threads llvmpipe-1 and
# llvmpipe-2). The driver's entry did not hide it: lavapipe's frames are only in the stacks that created the two
# threads, and a race report's thread-creation stacks are not matched against suppressions (compiler-rt 18,
# tsan_rtl_report.cpp: AddThread leaves them not suppressable). The same job passed present_test on PRs #63 and #68 and
# on native at 97eb0dd (run 41255452035), with the same file. So LLVM gets a race: entry too.
#
# They name only those three libraries: the loader, the software driver and the LLVM it loads (our binaries do not link
# LLVM). Never add a frame of plume (native/third_party/rhi/plume/patched) or of our code here: a report that has one is
# a race in the code under test (the control D2 of native/tests/rhi_plume_patterns_test.cpp makes one on purpose and is
# not run by the job), and the fix is the code.
#
# What they cost: a report with a frame in the loader, in lavapipe's driver or in its LLVM is hidden, so the job does not
# catch an unlocked Vulkan call that races inside the driver (a queue used from two threads without its lock, say). The
# guards for that are the validation layer's synchronization validation, the owner-rule checks and MH4N_RHI_CHECKS.
#
# They are `race:` and `mutex:` entries, not `called_from_lib:`: a called_from_lib entry for either library made
# ThreadSanitizer worse here (in one run it stopped the process with "library ... is unloaded" because the loader dlcloses
# the driver; in others it reported a data race inside libLLVM.so, a lock-order inversion, or "unlock of an unlocked
# mutex", in code that is clean without the entry; a data race inside libLLVM.so later came without such an entry too,
# in run 41252057160 above).

# the Vulkan loader and lavapipe's driver library, as a frame of a report
race:libvulkan.so
race:libvulkan_lvp.so

# the LLVM lavapipe loads, as a frame of a report (a substring: it matches the versioned file names above)
race:libLLVM

# the loader's own mutexes
mutex:libvulkan.so
